Author: Danny Milosavljevic <dannym@friendly-machines.com>
Date: 2026-04-20
Subject: Depack internal i386 backend records for the tp2cc-translated bootstrap (fpc 0.99)
License: expat

Same fix as fpc-1.0.6-c-record-packing.patch, applied to the fpc-0.99.14
source tree (where the affected records live in compiler/cpubase.pas --
the i386 back-end isn't split into its own subdir yet).

These records carry no file-format role -- they're internal i386
backend state and tables, not written to PPU files or to disk in any
other form.  Upstream declared them `packed' purely to save RAM on
1990s-era builds.

tp2cc translates Pascal `packed record' to `struct [[gnu::packed]]' in
the translated bootstrap.  Unlike `#pragma pack(push,1)', the attribute form makes GCC flag
latent UB that the pragma silently permits: in particular, binding a
C++ reference to a misaligned packed field (which is exactly what a
Pascal `var' parameter pointing at `treference.offset' or
`tlocation.register' becomes under the translated lowering).  Those bindings
assume natural alignment for the referent type; the packed-member
address carries no such guarantee, so GCC rejects them with
`cannot bind packed field X to T&'. For aggregate carriers such as
`tinsentry.optypes' and `TInsProp.Ch', the translated bootstrap will happily compile the
packed field access and then execute through a misaligned
subobject/reference at runtime.

On x86 the original FPC code worked because the CPU tolerates
unaligned loads, but under the translated bootstrap's strict reference rules the resulting references
are UB regardless of hardware.  Depacking these in-memory records
restores natural alignment without disturbing any on-disk layout, and
lets the [[gnu::packed]] policy keep catching real misalignments
wherever they might appear for records that *are* file formats
(`ppuheader', `tsymbol', `tarhdr' et al., which stay packed).

--- a/compiler/cpubase.pas
+++ b/compiler/cpubase.pas
@@ -425,7 +425,11 @@
 
   { immediate/reference record }
   preference = ^treference;
-  treference = packed record
+  { was `packed record' -- depacked for the tp2cc-translated bootstrap. This record
+    is in-memory-only (no file-format role), and packing it forced misaligned
+    access to its `longint'/pointer fields. C++ references to those fields
+    are UB when misaligned, which `[[gnu::packed]]' rightly rejects. }
+  treference = record
      is_immediate : boolean; { is this used as reference or immediate }
      segment,
      base,
@@ -477,7 +481,8 @@
   );
 
   plocation = ^tlocation;
-  tlocation = packed record
+  { depacked for tp2cc bootstrap, see note on `treference' above. }
+  tlocation = record
      case loc : tloc of
         LOC_MEM,LOC_REFERENCE : (reference : treference);
         LOC_FPU : ();
@@ -546,7 +551,13 @@
 
 {$ifndef NOAG386BIN}
 type
-  tinsentry=packed record
+  { was `packed record' -- depacked for the tp2cc-translated bootstrap. This
+    instruction-table entry is in-memory-only, and packing it misaligns
+    the `longint' operand-type array. tp2cc lowers that array to a translated
+    aggregate carrier whose indexed element access binds references to
+    the underlying elements; keeping this record packed therefore turns
+    normal operand-table lookups into misaligned-reference UB. }
+  tinsentry=record
     opcode  : tasmop;
     ops     : byte;
     optypes : array[0..2] of longint;
@@ -617,7 +628,11 @@
 const
   MaxCh = 3; { Max things a instruction can change }
 type
-  TInsProp = packed record
+  { was `packed record' -- depacked for the tp2cc-translated bootstrap. This is
+    optimizer metadata, not an on-disk format, and its change-array is
+    only consulted in memory. Keeping it packed forces aggregate indexed
+    access through a packed subobject for no file-format benefit. }
+  TInsProp = record
     Ch : Array[1..MaxCh] of TInsChange;
   end;
 
